1
00:00:00,545 --> 00:00:05,761
This is VerifiQuant, governance by execution for financial LLM workflows.

2
00:00:05,852 --> 00:00:10,380
It is joint work by Yu-Chi Ko, Mi-Yen Yeh and Shou-De Lin,

3
00:00:10,416 --> 00:00:14,669
at National Taiwan University and Academia Sinica.

4
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:18,988
The one-line version is: keep the spreadsheet, and let the LLM fill it in.

5
00:00:20,033 --> 00:00:24,058
LLMs in finance now sit in workflows that end in a number.

6
00:00:24,149 --> 00:00:27,992
Valuation memos, client calculators, regulatory reports.

7
00:00:28,082 --> 00:00:30,094
They rarely fail by crashing.

8
00:00:30,185 --> 00:00:33,972
They fail with a clean, wrong number, and nothing marks it as wrong.

9
00:00:34,062 --> 00:00:38,672
Program-aided reasoning fixes the arithmetic, because an interpreter runs the code.

10
00:00:38,762 --> 00:00:42,802
But it does not decide whether the question justifies a number at all.

11
00:00:43,847 --> 00:00:46,775
Here is a real trap from our evaluation.

12
00:00:46,866 --> 00:00:50,222
It asks for a stock’s price rate of change over one week.

13
00:00:50,313 --> 00:00:54,818
We changed the opening price from 150 dollars to 2.5 dollars.

14
00:00:54,909 --> 00:00:59,950
R-PAL sees the same retrieved contracts and runs code. It reports 6,500 percent.

15
00:01:00,041 --> 00:01:04,499
R-PAL plus also gets every one of our checks described in its prompt.

16
00:01:04,590 --> 00:01:08,690
It still reports 6,500 percent, in all three runs.

17
00:01:08,781 --> 00:01:11,634
VerifiQuant stops at gate E in all three runs,

18
00:01:11,671 --> 00:01:16,575
and shows the check that failed: a weekly price change above 200 percent.

19
00:01:16,666 --> 00:01:22,503
Same model, same contracts. The difference is whether the check is executed or only described.

20
00:01:23,549 --> 00:01:26,975
So the question we ask is not how accurate the system is.

21
00:01:27,066 --> 00:01:29,924
It is when a number may leave the system at all.

22
00:01:30,014 --> 00:01:33,727
Every response is one of three things: a correct number,

23
00:01:33,764 --> 00:01:37,162
a silent wrong, or a named exit that says why.

24
00:01:37,253 --> 00:01:40,261
The goal is to turn silent wrongs into named exits,

25
00:01:40,297 --> 00:01:43,601
while keeping as many correct numbers as possible.

26
00:01:44,646 --> 00:01:49,473
There are five reasons a question may not justify a number, and each gets a letter.

27
00:01:49,564 --> 00:01:52,649
N: nothing in the library covers the question.

28
00:01:52,740 --> 00:01:58,306
M: the intent is unclear, like “the return” without saying holding-period or annualized.

29
00:01:58,397 --> 00:02:01,054
F: a required input is missing.

30
00:02:01,145 --> 00:02:05,701
E: a value breaks a declared check, like that 2.5-dollar price.

31
00:02:05,792 --> 00:02:10,998
I: a convention is left open, like whether payments come at the start or the end of each period.

32
00:02:11,089 --> 00:02:14,043
These letters are also the gates of the pipeline.

33
00:02:15,088 --> 00:02:18,260
To catch them, we borrow from how analysts already work.

34
00:02:18,351 --> 00:02:22,219
An analyst does not re-derive a loan formula for each client.

35
00:02:22,310 --> 00:02:27,540
They open a vetted sheet, type in the inputs, and the sheet refuses impossible values.

36
00:02:27,631 --> 00:02:33,034
We write each formula family once as such a sheet. We call it a Financial Inference Contract.

37
00:02:33,125 --> 00:02:34,894
The library holds 250 of them,

38
00:02:34,930 --> 00:02:39,883
generated offline and checked with lint and smoke tests. None was hand-tuned.

39
00:02:39,974 --> 00:02:43,388
The LLM gets three jobs, and only three.

40
00:02:43,478 --> 00:02:48,943
Pick the sheet. Fill the input cells. Flag which declared conventions the text leaves open.

41
00:02:49,034 --> 00:02:53,843
It never computes the answer, and it never freely rewrites the formula.

42
00:02:54,888 --> 00:02:58,479
This is the real loan contract, drawn as a sheet.

43
00:02:58,570 --> 00:03:02,867
The orange rows are what the LLM fills: principal, rate and term.

44
00:03:02,958 --> 00:03:04,989
The blue rows are data validation,

45
00:03:05,026 --> 00:03:10,086
run as code. The principal must be positive, and the rate must stay below 1.0.

46
00:03:10,177 --> 00:03:12,280
Then come declared conventions.

47
00:03:12,371 --> 00:03:16,710
Rate scale is hard, because it changes the computation, so the system asks.

48
00:03:16,801 --> 00:03:20,188
Payment timing is soft, because it changes how you read the number,

49
00:03:20,225 --> 00:03:22,178
so the system answers and warns.

50
00:03:22,269 --> 00:03:27,891
The green row is locked Python code, which returns 1,498.88.

51
00:03:27,982 --> 00:03:33,351
Now suppose the LLM binds “6 percent” as 6 instead of 0.06.

52
00:03:33,442 --> 00:03:38,970
The rate check fails, and the query ends at an alert that names the check. No number leaves.

53
00:03:40,016 --> 00:03:44,308
At run time, every query goes through this funnel, from left to right.

54
00:03:44,399 --> 00:03:48,187
Retrieval is BM25 over the contracts, top four.

55
00:03:48,278 --> 00:03:53,061
Then three LLM steps, in orange, alternate with code steps, in blue.

56
00:03:53,152 --> 00:03:57,659
Select picks one contract, or none. Bind fills the inputs.

57
00:03:57,750 --> 00:04:03,191
Code then checks that every required input is present, and that every declared boundary holds.

58
00:04:03,282 --> 00:04:06,558
The ambiguity step asks whether a convention is still open.

59
00:04:06,649 --> 00:04:10,781
Only a query that passes every gate reaches the contract’s own code.

60
00:04:11,827 --> 00:04:13,985
Every stop is a named exit.

61
00:04:14,076 --> 00:04:20,330
N refuses. M asks which quantity you meant. F asks for the missing field by name.

62
00:04:20,421 --> 00:04:23,269
E names the failed check and the bound value.

63
00:04:23,360 --> 00:04:26,906
For conventions, a soft one returns the number with a warning.

64
00:04:26,997 --> 00:04:31,966
A hard one asks the user to pick a declared option, and then applies a verified edit.

65
00:04:32,057 --> 00:04:34,315
Every path, with a number or without one,

66
00:04:34,351 --> 00:04:38,195
writes a DiagnosticReport that replays from the stored binding.

67
00:04:39,241 --> 00:04:41,058
Now the main result.

68
00:04:41,149 --> 00:04:45,414
We derived 80 traps from the contracts: 20 each of missing input,

69
00:04:45,451 --> 00:04:49,211
impossible value, open convention, and out of scope.

70
00:04:49,301 --> 00:04:52,329
We count how often a system still gives an unqualified

71
00:04:52,366 --> 00:04:55,294
number. We call this false numeric acceptance.

72
00:04:55,385 --> 00:04:58,440
R-PAL plus has our whole checklist in its prompt,

73
00:04:58,476 --> 00:05:01,759
and it still answers 27.5 percent of the traps.

74
00:05:01,850 --> 00:05:06,693
VerifiQuant runs the same checks in stages, and answers 10.4 percent.

75
00:05:06,784 --> 00:05:12,634
That is 17 points lower, with a 95 percent interval from about 7 to 27 points.

76
00:05:12,725 --> 00:05:18,965
Every system uses Gemini 2.5 Flash and the same top-four contracts, averaged over three runs.

77
00:05:20,011 --> 00:05:23,938
Where does the gap come from? Boundary and ambiguity traps.

78
00:05:24,029 --> 00:05:30,540
On impossible values, R-PAL plus accepts 30 percent, and VerifiQuant 8.3.

79
00:05:30,631 --> 00:05:34,204
On open conventions, 80 percent against 21.7.

80
00:05:34,294 --> 00:05:39,416
There, the right answer depends on an executed check or an unstated convention.

81
00:05:39,507 --> 00:05:42,022
On missing inputs and out-of-scope questions,

82
00:05:42,059 --> 00:05:45,495
a prompt already refuses, and VerifiQuant is no better.

83
00:05:45,586 --> 00:05:51,417
Plain R-PAL, which only runs code, answers 48.8 percent of all traps.

84
00:05:52,463 --> 00:05:54,887
This is not an accuracy result.

85
00:05:54,978 --> 00:06:00,579
On 250 well-posed questions, R-PAL reaches 96.8 percent accuracy.

86
00:06:00,670 --> 00:06:05,596
VerifiQuant reaches 81.2 percent, because it declines about 16 percent of them.

87
00:06:05,687 --> 00:06:10,770
Its silent-wrong rate stays close to R-PAL’s, 2.8 against 2.4 percent.

88
00:06:10,861 --> 00:06:14,338
The grey row is an upper bound, not a deployment result.

89
00:06:14,429 --> 00:06:19,505
There, an oracle that knows the intended computation, but never the answer,

90
00:06:19,541 --> 00:06:23,948
may clarify up to twice. Accuracy then reaches 93.6 percent.

91
00:06:24,994 --> 00:06:28,227
This chart puts safety and utility on one picture.

92
00:06:28,318 --> 00:06:31,150
Coverage on clean questions runs across. False

93
00:06:31,186 --> 00:06:35,120
acceptance on traps runs up. The ideal is the lower right.

94
00:06:35,211 --> 00:06:39,992
The two hollow points test whether the gain comes only from splitting the prompt into stages.

95
00:06:40,084 --> 00:06:45,009
Staging alone reaches 15.8 percent, but only by refusing much more.

96
00:06:45,100 --> 00:06:48,800
Staging plus the contract text ties VerifiQuant on traps,

97
00:06:48,837 --> 00:06:53,220
at 8.8 percent, but covers only 75 percent of clean questions.

98
00:06:53,310 --> 00:06:56,267
So staging over the declared contract lowers false acceptance,

99
00:06:56,303 --> 00:06:59,083
and running the contract’s own code keeps coverage.

100
00:06:59,174 --> 00:07:00,908
Measured against R-PAL plus,

101
00:07:00,945 --> 00:07:05,626
each trap number avoided costs VerifiQuant 2.0 correct clean answers.

102
00:07:06,671 --> 00:07:11,168
One more technical piece: what happens when a user picks a non-default convention.

103
00:07:11,258 --> 00:07:14,745
The computation has to change. If the LLM rewrote the code,

104
00:07:14,781 --> 00:07:17,966
it would reopen the door the funnel just closed.

105
00:07:18,057 --> 00:07:21,421
So every edit is declared on the contract at build time.

106
00:07:21,512 --> 00:07:26,844
For the loan, paying at the start of each period means dividing by one plus the monthly rate.

107
00:07:26,935 --> 00:07:31,428
The invariant says: multiply back, and you recover the old payment.

108
00:07:31,519 --> 00:07:38,488
The payment moves from 1,498.88 to 1,491.42.

109
00:07:38,578 --> 00:07:40,760
Before the edit runs, three checks.

110
00:07:40,851 --> 00:07:44,408
Bounded: 13 syntax-tree nodes, the declared limit.

111
00:07:44,498 --> 00:07:47,186
Safe: whitelisted names only.

112
00:07:47,277 --> 00:07:51,177
Matches: the invariant holds on five sampled inputs.

113
00:07:52,222 --> 00:07:56,870
To test the verifier, we generated 906 faulty patches on purpose.

114
00:07:56,961 --> 00:07:59,868
27 turned out to be equivalent to the original.

115
00:07:59,959 --> 00:08:06,469
Of the other 879, the verifier rejects 97.7 percent, including every structural attack.

116
00:08:06,560 --> 00:08:08,158
20 get through.

117
00:08:08,248 --> 00:08:11,636
This is bounded numerical validation, not a formal proof.

118
00:08:11,727 --> 00:08:16,218
Most of the 20 sit in branches that none of the sampled inputs reached.

119
00:08:17,264 --> 00:08:20,062
Outside the library, we ran two separate tests.

120
00:08:20,153 --> 00:08:24,063
First, we rebuilt the library from 50 formula families,

121
00:08:24,100 --> 00:08:27,504
so 199 questions have no matching contract.

122
00:08:27,595 --> 00:08:30,822
VerifiQuant abstains on 88.9 percent of them.

123
00:08:30,913 --> 00:08:35,773
R-PAL abstains on half a percent. It is right 95 percent of the time from memory,

124
00:08:35,810 --> 00:08:38,365
but gives no signal when it is wrong.

125
00:08:38,456 --> 00:08:43,571
So out of library, VerifiQuant is not more accurate. What it offers is a scope signal.

126
00:08:43,662 --> 00:08:48,743
Second, five finance-trained annotators labeled 80 natural queries blind.

127
00:08:48,834 --> 00:08:52,155
On the 46 that call for no number, the ordering holds,

128
00:08:52,191 --> 00:08:55,431
but only the gap to plain R-PAL is significant.

129
00:08:56,477 --> 00:08:58,000
To be clear about the boundary.

130
00:08:58,091 --> 00:08:59,799
The claim holds for single-number,

131
00:08:59,836 --> 00:09:03,797
formula-grounded questions inside the library, with a correct contract.

132
00:09:03,887 --> 00:09:07,551
The silent wrongs that remain trace to three LLM steps:

133
00:09:07,587 --> 00:09:11,067
selection, binding, and recalling a convention.

134
00:09:11,157 --> 00:09:14,908
The gates check schema and bounds, not where a value came from.

135
00:09:14,999 --> 00:09:19,261
And this is not a compliance claim. Traceable records are a design target.

136
00:09:20,307 --> 00:09:23,922
So: keep the spreadsheet, and let the LLM fill it in.

137
00:09:24,013 --> 00:09:28,758
It pays wherever an unflagged wrong number costs more than a declined question,

138
00:09:28,794 --> 00:09:32,622
as in valuation, reporting, and client-facing calculation.

139
00:09:32,713 --> 00:09:35,833
If coverage is all you need, R-PAL is the better choice.

140
00:09:35,924 --> 00:09:40,308
The live demo at verifiquant.com replays every trap from the paper.

141
00:09:40,399 --> 00:09:45,295
The QR codes on screen link to the demo, the code, and the paper. Thank you.
